---
import Section from './Section.astro';
import { site } from '../site';

const steps = [
  {
    title: 'Point it at a collection',
    text: 'A folder, an S3 bucket or a parquet manifest of images. A profile of the collection first shows what it holds, what is possible and what indexing will cost.',
    points: [
      'Reads images where they are and never copies the originals',
      'Needs no metadata. An existing catalog groups images into objects, links each one to its record and makes the records searchable',
      'Splits a large collection into groups, such as the museums of one institution, that visitors can filter by',
    ],
  },
  {
    title: 'Build the index',
    text: 'An open multimodal model embeds every image on a machine you control. No image is sent to an external API.',
    points: [
      'Scales to hundreds of thousands of images',
      'Resumes where it left off after an interruption',
      'Index on a large machine, then serve from a small one',
    ],
  },
  {
    title: 'Open the search site',
    text: 'Visitors search in natural language or with an image, across photographs, maps, documents and 3D models. Where a catalog exists, search weighs what the images show against what the records say, and tells the visitor how it read the query.',
    points: [
      'Installs only what the collection holds: 3D models, PDFs and deep zoom are options',
      'Runs on a laptop, your own server or AWS, at a domain of its own or under a path of your website. Serving needs no GPU',
      'Upload and rate limits protect a public site, and every refusal is explained',
      'Describes itself to search engines, and answers AI assistants through its own MCP server',
    ],
  },
];

const papers = {
  siglip: 'https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.15343',
  siglip2: 'https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.14786',
  clip: 'https://arxiv.org/abs/2103.00020',
};
---

<Section id="how" eyebrow="How it works" title="From a collection to a search site in three steps">
  <ol class="steps">
    {steps.map((step, i) => (
      <li>
        <span class="num" aria-hidden="true">{i + 1}</span>
        <h3>{step.title}</h3>
        <p>{step.text}</p>
        <ul>{step.points.map((point) => <li>{point}</li>)}</ul>
      </li>
    ))}
  </ol>
  <p class="models">
    Embeddings come from open models that run on your own machine or cloud account: <a href={papers.siglip} rel="noopener">SigLIP</a> by default, <a href={papers.siglip2} rel="noopener">SigLIP 2</a> or <a href={papers.clip} rel="noopener">CLIP</a>. An index can hold vectors from several, and the site searches with the one you choose.
  </p>
  <p class="ask">Want another model? <a href={site.ideasUrl} rel="noopener">Ask for it on the wishlist.</a></p>
</Section>

<style>
  .steps { list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); gap: 32px; }
  @media (min-width: 960px) {
    .steps { grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: 40px; }
  }
  .steps > li { margin: 0; padding-top: 20px; border-top: 2px solid var(--line); }
  .num {
    display: inline-grid;
    place-items: center;
    width: 36px;
    height: 36px;
    border-radius: 50%;
    background: var(--accent);
    color: var(--accent-ink);
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 700;
    margin-bottom: 14px;
  }
  .steps h3 { font-size: 1.15rem; margin-bottom: 0.4em; }
  .steps p { margin-bottom: 0.75em; }
  .steps ul { margin: 0; padding-left: 1.1em; list-style: disc; color: var(--ink-soft); font-size: 0.95rem; }
  .steps ul li { margin-bottom: 0.3em; }
  .models { margin: 40px 0 0; padding-top: 20px; border-top: 1px solid var(--line); color: var(--ink-soft); }
  .ask { margin: 12px 0 0; color: var(--ink-soft); }
</style>
